Paper 1: 10.1016_j.jrt.2026.100183 (2026)                                                                      

Title: AGI for healthcare? A call for conceptual discipline in the European Union                              

Analysis: Recent policy discussions in the European Union have highlighted the need for conceptual discipline  
when addressing Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) in healthcare. The authors argue that the term AGI is    
contested and potentially risky, suggesting that its use in funding calls may conflict with established        
precautionary principles. This underscores the growing tension between rapid technological advancement and     
regulatory frameworks [^10.1016_j.jrt.2026.100183].                                                            

Paper 2: 10.5281_zenodo.19034234 (2026)                                                                        

Title: The Topology of Mind: Morphogenetic Continuity and the Architecture of Autonomous General Intelligence  

Analysis: This work proposes a foundational shift in cognitive science by replacing the subjective unit with a 
"Form F" to describe the transition from computation to autonomy. It frames consciousness as the systemic      
capacity to integrate stochastic perturbations into a stable gradient, mathematically linking autonomy and     
survival instincts to the preservation of topological integrity. This approach establishes a rigorous geometric
framework for understanding the emergence of AGI [^10.5281_zenodo.19034234].                                   

Paper 3: 10.22339_jbh.v7i4.7403 (2024)                                                                         

Title: Robot Consciousness                                                                                     

Analysis: The intersection of logic, metamathematics, and brain sciences has renewed interest in artificial    
consciousness. This paper explores conjectures regarding sufficiently advanced general robotic intelligence,   
questioning whether it would be indistinguishable from human consciousness. It further examines the            
implications of such systems in responsible roles and their relationship to broader theories of mind,          
emergence, and free will [^10.22339_jbh.v7i4.7403].                                                            

Paper 4: 10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636 (2024)                                                                         

Title: The TESCREAL bundle: Eugenics and the promise of utopia through artificial general intelligence         

Analysis: Critical analysis of the AGI development movement reveals deep normative roots in the Anglo-American 
eugenics tradition. The authors argue that framing AGI as an unquestioned goal for "safety" and "benefiting    
humanity" masks discriminatory attitudes that could centralize power and harm marginalized groups. This        
perspective urges a shift toward developing defined, testable systems rather than pursuing an all-knowing AGI  
[^10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636].                                                                                     

Paper 5: 10.48550_arxiv.2309.01933 (2023)                                                                      

Title: Provably safe systems: the only path to controllable AGI                                                

Analysis: This paper outlines a pathway to safely deploying powerful AGIs by ensuring they provably satisfy    
human-specified requirements. It argues that advanced AI for formal verification and mechanistic               
interpretability will soon make this technically feasible, presenting it as the only guaranteed path to        
controllable AGI. The authors propose a set of challenge problems to advance this critical safety research     
[^10.48550_arxiv.2309.01933].                                                                                  

Paper 6: 10.51593_20210064 (2022)                                                                              

Title: China's Advanced AI Research: Monitoring China's Paths to "General" Artificial Intelligence             

Analysis: China's national strategy explicitly targets leadership in "general AI" capable of autonomous action 
in novel circumstances by 2030. Open-source research identifies numerous institutions engaged in machine       
learning, brain-inspired AI, and brain-computer interfaces. This report highlights the geopolitical race to    
achieve AGI and the mechanisms in place to track progress and provide timely alerts [^10.51593_20210064].      

Paper 7: 10.1016_j.intell.2021.101563 (2021)                                                                   

Title: The future of intelligence research in the coming age of artificial intelligence – With a special       
consideration of the philosophical movements of trans- and posthumanism                                        

Analysis: This contribution examines the intersection of human intelligence research, cognitive enhancements,  
and the philosophical movements of trans- and posthumanism. It discusses the promises and perils of developing 
an artificial "super-intelligence," outlining how psychology and individual differences should inform the      
development of such systems to ensure they align with human values and societal needs                          
[^10.1016_j.intell.2021.101563].                                                                               

